{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-159866",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i};\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given a sample of $n=8$ independent Bernoulli random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta .$ Therefore, the probability mass function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )={\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{1-x},\\,x=0,1.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{i}}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡((1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{i}}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(1-{x}_{i})\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ){\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(1-{x}_{i}) \\\\  & =S\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(n-S)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ is the number of successes in the sample.\n\nFinally, since there are $2$ successes in the sample, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(8-2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
